{"name":"Grey Matter","hex":"#c87f89","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"grey matter","whole":true,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["Brains; a person's ability to think.","Literal brains as a general substance."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/grey_matter"}],"entries":[{"title":"Gray Matter (band)","blocks":[{"text":"Gray Matter was an American post-hardcore band from Washington, D.C., United States, who played in the 1980s and 1990s. They disbanded in 1986, but reformed in 1990 before disbanding again in 1993."}],"link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Gray_Matter_(band)","wikidata":"Q1172137"},{"title":"Grey Matter (album)","blocks":[{"text":"Grey Matter is the début full-length album from Canadian indie-rock band Wool on Wolves. It was self-released on November 9, 2010 and produced by Nik Kozub of Shout Out Out Out Out."}],"image":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/en/f/f7/Wool_on_Wolves_-_Grey_Matter.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Grey_Matter_(album)","wikidata":"Q5608205"},{"title":"Grey matter","blocks":[{"text":"Grey matter is a major component of the central nervous system, consisting of neuronal cell bodies, neuropil, glial cells, synapses, and capillaries. Grey matter is distinguished from white matter in that it contains numerous cell bodies and relatively few myelinated axons, while white matter contains relatively few cell bodies and is composed chiefly of long-range myelinated axons. The colour difference arises mainly from the whiteness of myelin. In living tissue, grey matter actually has a very light grey colour with yellowish or pinkish hues, which come from capillary blood vessels and neuronal cell bodies."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d2/Spinal_nerve.svg/330px-Spinal_nerve.svg.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Grey_matter","wikidata":"Q646150"},{"title":"Matter","blocks":[{"text":"In physical science, matter is anything that has mass and occupies space by having volume. All everyday objects that can be touched are ultimately composed of atoms, which are made up of interacting subatomic particles. In everyday as well as scientific usage, matter generally includes atoms and anything made up of them, and any particles that act as if they have both rest mass and volume. However it does not include massless particles such as photons, or other energy phenomena or waves such as light or heat. Matter exists in various states. These include classical everyday phases such as solid, liquid, and gas – for example water exists as ice, liquid water, and gaseous steam – but other states are possible, including plasma, Bose–Einstein condensates, fermionic condensates, and quark–gluon plasma."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/83/Hydrogen_discharge_tube.jpg/330px-Hydrogen_discharge_tube.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Matter","wikidata":"Q35758"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d7/Grey_matter_and_white_matter_-_very_high_mag.jpg/500px-Grey_matter_and_white_matter_-_very_high_mag.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d7/Grey_matter_and_white_matter_-_very_high_mag.jpg/1280px-Grey_matter_and_white_matter_-_very_high_mag.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d7/Grey_matter_and_white_matter_-_very_high_mag.jpg/500px-Grey_matter_and_white_matter_-_very_high_mag.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AGrey_matter_and_white_matter_-_very_high_mag.jpg"},{"srcset":"ht